            <ArticleTitle>Characterization of polymer chain fractions of kiwifruit starch.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>579-587</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0308-8146(17)31232-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.foodchem.2017.07.079</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this report, the amylose composition and molecular structure of starches from the core and outer pericarp of 3 golden kiwifruit varieties were characterised, using enzymatic and chromatographic techniques. Starches from the core tissues of kiwifruit tend to have higher amylose contents (by ∼3-5%) and longer unit chains of both amylopectins and their φ, β-limit dextrins (LDs) than those of the outer pericarp starches. The contents of short B-chains of the φ, β-LDs of amylopectins from the outer pericarp were higher (by ∼3%) than those of φ, β-LDs of the core amylopectins. Overall, the composition and structure of starches from the outer pericarp and core tissues of a golden kiwifruit were different. This study provides a structural basis to further investigate the starch degradation in kiwifruit, which may be of importance for the storage and eating quality of the fruit.</AbstractText>
